    aboutRole: ["Marketing managers own how a product or brand reaches and persuades its audience. They sit at the intersection of creativity and analytics: shaping the message and the campaigns that carry it, then measuring what actually moves customers to act. The role spans a wide range depending on the company: brand marketing (building awareness and identity), product marketing (positioning a product and arming sales), growth and performance marketing (running paid channels and optimizing conversion), and content or lifecycle marketing. A marketing manager typically owns a budget, a set of channels, and a number to hit, coordinating designers, writers, agencies, and analysts to hit it. It is one of the most common paths for creative-but-commercial graduates, and one of the few business roles judged on both taste and measurable results."],
